   "body": "/claim cloud/i970-sonnet\n\nPlan:\n- `AppVersion` gains the git commit (`HFRGitCommit` Info.plist key, `?` when absent), a `BuildReport` value (OS, device model, simulator model via `SIMULATOR_MODEL_IDENTIFIER`) and a pure formatter `SwiftHFR <version> (<build>) · <commit> · <OS> · <model>`; Swift Testing tests cover a missing key, a missing commit and a simulator model.\n- Version rows (Réglages and À propos) become a `Button` that copies the report, shows « Copié » and posts a VoiceOver announcement; the row shows `1.0 (42) · cd04954`.\n- Commit injection: see the question below. No new dependency, nothing secret, only `git rev-parse`.\n- Draft PR with `compile-check`, Opus adversarial review, then the ready request on #893.\n\n**Question before I touch the build settings.** Today no build phase exists, and the target has `ENABLE_USER_SCRIPT_SANDBOXING = YES`: a Run Script phase cannot run `git rev-parse` under it (`.git` is outside the declared inputs). The script phase you describe therefore needs `ENABLE_USER_SCRIPT_SANDBOXING = NO` on the `SwiftHFR` target (a build-security setting change in `project.pbxproj`, ADR 0012 « reviewed on its own »). Do you accept that, or do you prefer a sandbox-safe route (a CI/local pre-build step writing an untracked `Config/GitCommit.xcconfig`, which `Info-Keys.plist` reads as `$(HFR_GIT_COMMIT)`)? Until you answer I do the Swift side, which works with either, and the PR will be `Refs #970` with the injection left.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
